1.2 Fundamental Postulates of TDMU

The theoretical basis of this paper is the Topological Dynamic Membrane Universe (TDMU 2.32) framework, which rests on the following postulates:

Ontological postulate: There exists a discrete, background-free combinatorial network consisting of nodes and directed edges. This network is not embedded in any pre-existing space or time. The only primitive relation between nodes and edges is “connected” or “not connected.” The only primitive attribute of an edge is its direction. The only dynamics of the network is local reconnection—when two edges meet at a node, they may disconnect and reconnect according to directional rules. The directional rules ensure that the difference between the number of counterclockwise-directed edges and clockwise-directed edges along any closed loop remains invariant.

Definition of matter: A woven subgraph consisting of loops that does not spontaneously disintegrate under the reconnection dynamics is called a topological knot. Matter is topological knots. A primitive brick is the smallest topological knot on the membrane that can exist independently and can establish stable shared links with other topological knots. The mass of a primitive brick is defined as its resistance to directional reconnection—i.e., the number of reconnection events required to change the net directional algebraic sum of its loops.

Definition of interaction: Multiple connecting paths established between two topological knots via intermediate loops on the membrane are called shared links. Shared links are the sole medium of interaction between topological knots. No independent “force” entity exists. All interactions are manifestations of the geometric properties of shared links. Gravity is the large-number statistical effect of the total number of shared links. Light is the coupled transverse wave of shared-link density waves and orientation-degree waves. Magnetism is the macroscopic torsional stress field arising from the spatially non-uniform distribution of the orientation degree of shared links. Electric force is the normal stress wave of the shared-link density gradient. The strong nuclear force is the nonlinear saturation behavior of shared links at extremely short range. The weak interaction is the spontaneous decay mode of metastable shared-link networks.

