6. Unification of the Two Extreme Appearances

By varying the envelope mass-supply rate and angular-momentum distribution, a continuous observational sequence can be produced, ranging from blue supergiants to red supergiants:

Mass-extreme stars (blue supergiants, e.g., R136a1): very high envelope supply rate, dense and hot surface; Path I burning concentrated in a relatively compact region.
Volume-extreme stars (red supergiants, e.g., Stephenson 2-18): lower envelope supply rate or fully expanded, rarefied and cool surface; Path I burning distributed over a vast volume.
Both are optical manifestations of the same “black-hole + accretion envelope” system under different boundary conditions. The distribution of massive stars on the HR diagram is not an evolutionary sequence trajectory but a continuous sampling of envelope parameters along the mass coordinate.

